      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Academic Integrity in a Culture of Cheating</h2>
      <p>Lindsey Konken, Student Activities Director</p>
      <p>The statistics are beyond stag-gering. In some studies, up to 80% of high-achieving high school stu-dents admit to cheating. 21% of students who say that cheating is “unacceptable” still engage in cheat-ing behaviors. More than half of teenagers say they have cheated on a test during the last year. 34% have done it more than once.</p>
      <p>The methods can be more cur-ious than the statistics. Pho-toshop lessons on YouTube create fake soda bottle labels. Rubber bands stretched to their limits hold notes unnoticed around a campus. Students offer to professionally write essays for a small fee.</p>
      <p>If it were just about the ease of accessing information, maybe more of us would cheat. After all, technol-ogy makes it easier than ever with more information at our fingertips. But then and there. And cheating is a pretty low-risk way to try to get ahead, especially when you are under pressure, actually ending up with higher grades than non-cheaters. So what keeps us from cheating? It’s not risk, con-sequences, or methods?</p>
      <p>How we view academics and cheating must be closely linked to how we view the world around us, to our beliefs and values. When we connect what we believe to how we behave and make that connection in all areas of life, even little cheats and tricks to help us pass a test or fill an essay might suddenly tug at our sense of right and wrong.</p>
      <p>The Wendt Scholars, in a re-cent evening session, discussed what values influenced their actions in</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 70%; padding-left: 40px;">
      <p>regard to academic integrity, noting that it is a matter of character, of integrity, the golden rule, respect, honesty, and the pursuit of truth. The University also holds policies such as the academic integrity policy. The value of integrity encom-passes “living lives that are true; lives that are true, lives that are whole, and grounded in the Christian tradi-tion,” in all areas of life, even our aca-demic lives.”</p>
      <p>“http://researchnews.osu.edu/archive/student-handbook”</p>
      <p>— UD Student Handbook</p>
      <p>Wendt Scholars reflect on UD’s academic integrity policy and the idea of an honor code.</p>
      <p>“Freshman Megan Loas said, “these are the rules that have been expected of us since we came to UD. I honestly would have been disappointed if the school expected anything less.”</p>
      <p>Senior Monica Klima, learned something new in her review of the “non-permitted collaboration on assign-ments” policy. “I guess now I know that I can’t just copy from the internet to ask the professor if it will be okay to use it in my paper. I have to be honest.”</p>
      <p>Seminarian Andy Sommers com-mented on an honor code: “I do be-lieve that it is possible to make a difference to make a difference. In our society today, honor seems less valued. Society has become so focused on the material that it is inappropriate in many cases where evidence is rather clear that a given action is wrong. We should be a forgiving society but also one that holds one another responsible for our actions.”</p>
    </div>
