      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">A Good Life</h2>
      <p>Paul Jensen</p>
      <p>Earlier in the semester, Paul Jensen spoke to Wendt Scholars about the challenge of virtue in their cultural context. He shares of his comments here.</p>
      <p><strong>Do you have students who should be Wendt Scholars?</strong></p>
      <p>Wendt Scholar Applica-tions are due January 31 for the 2013-2014 Aca-demic Year.</p>
      <p>Applications and refer-rences can be found on the Wendt site (http://wendt.dbq.edu/apply/current.cfm).</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 70%; padding-left: 40px;">
      <p>an uncomfortably large number of American adults (18 to 23 year olds), display behaviors that degrade and impoverish them. Smith and his team doc-ument this in their book, <span data-field="book_title">{{ book_title }}</span>. Their scientific sampling confirms that emerging adults are morally adrift, captured by consumer-ism, frequently intoxi-cated, creators and vic-tims of the “hooking up” culture, and civically and politically disen-gaged. What’s to be done?</p>
      <p>Identifying degrading behavior is a first step, but something on the positive side of the leg-er is also required. In the past 30 years, moral philosophers, to their great credit, have receiv-ed the ancient tradi-tion of virtue – moral and intellectual human excellences acquired by action.</p>
      <p>From Plato and Aris-totle, Christian medieval philosophers identified four classical virtues – prudence, temperance, courage, and justice – and added to them the seven cardinal vir-tues. That these virtues have failed to make</p>
      <p>their way into the lives of emerging adults is tragic. However, despair is not an option. We can do more. We can do more to help.</p>
      <p>Philosopher John Hare has offered a simple mod-el of morality: there is a first moral dom-ain, followed by a second moral dom-ain, and then a third moral dom-ain. This gap is seen all too well in emerging adults, but it is a forgivable society but also one that holds one another responsible for our actions.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

